# Generated / derived output — all of it is regenerable from tools/, and
# out/wdf/sample/ additionally holds extracted game assets that should not
# live in version control.
out/

# Your working mod tree and staged assets (contains game art).
mods/

# Built Blender addon zip — regenerate with `py -3 tools/build_addon.py`.
build/

# Local server / capture run output. Runtime artefacts, and a live run can
# record gameplay state — keep it out of version control.
sessions/

# Third-party server emulators you clone to test against. CoEmu is GPL-3;
# vendoring it here would raise copyleft questions for this whole repository.
# It stays a separate project you install yourself — see docs/server_setup.md.
server/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/

# Per-machine Claude Code settings
.claude/settings.local.json

# Where YOUR copy of the game is installed. Written by
# `py -3 core/coroot.py --set DIR --scope repo`, and by the viewer's setup
# page. Machine-specific by definition -- never commit it.
.co-root

# Personal data belonging to whoever runs this. login_history.json holds
# account and character names; if you ever copy one here, it must not be
# committed. tests/test_sanitization.py fails the build if any of it lands in
# a tracked file. See docs/sanitization.md.
login_history.json
**/login_history.json

# YOUR OWN identifiers, in plaintext, for tests/test_sanitization.py to check
# against before you publish anything. One per line; see the README section
# "Protecting your own identifiers".
#
# This is per-contributor by design and must NEVER be committed -- not even as
# hashes. Character names and account handles are short and guessable, so a
# published hash of one is a verification oracle, not a protection. The test
# fails loudly if this file ever lands in git's index.
.sanitize-local

# YOUR game clients, and everything derived beside them. A client directory
# holds a whole install -- Conquer.exe, data.wdf, loose art -- and THIS IS A
# PUBLIC REPOSITORY whose one-sentence claim is that it ships no game data.
#
# This was enforced per-directory: each client folder carried its own
# `.gitignore` (`*`, `!.gitignore`, `!.gitkeep`). That protects only the
# folders somebody remembered to create the pair for, and on 2026-08-27 it
# failed twice in one day -- `4274` left 5,973 files visible, and `6271`
# left 70,045, each one `git add -A` away from publishing a game client.
#
# The rules below cover every client directory including the ones that do
# not exist yet, so a new install is protected the moment it is dropped in
# rather than when somebody remembers. The per-directory files stay: they are
# harmless, and they keep working if this block is ever edited away.
# Loose files dropped straight into Clients/ -- installers usually, before
# anyone has extracted them into a per-client folder. The rule below this
# one only covers files INSIDE a client directory, so seven 3.5GB
# installers sat git-visible here on 2026-08-28 until this was added.
# Directories are re-included so git still descends into real clients.
ConquerAssets/Clients/*
!ConquerAssets/Clients/*/
ConquerAssets/Clients/*/*
!ConquerAssets/Clients/*/.gitignore
!ConquerAssets/Clients/*/.gitkeep

ConquerAssets/derived/*
!ConquerAssets/derived/.gitignore
!ConquerAssets/derived/.gitkeep

# Sibling trees under ConquerAssets/ are the same kind of thing: extracted or
# variant game art. Named explicitly rather than with a catch-all so that a
# NEW sibling shows up as untracked and gets a decision, instead of being
# silently swallowed by a rule nobody revisits.
ConquerAssets/_variants/*

# Editor / OS noise
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.swp
